# Z numerals as first-class symbolic leaves, plus the N -> Z promotion
# that COMPLETES natural partiality: subtraction results and negations
# that do not exist over the naturals become zint terms.
#
# PREREQUISITES:
#   .import decimal-arithmetic  # or binary-arithmetic / binary-nand-arithmetic
#   .import integer-arithmetic  # incl. the uniform operator facade
#   .import symbolic-core
#   .import symbolic-minus
#   .import symbolic-integers
#
# ONE WORLD FOR THE NONNEGATIVE INTEGERS: a nonnegative value is always
# represented by the natural numeral, a zint term exclusively denotes a
# NEGATIVE value, (neg zint N) where N > 0. Every normal form generated
# by this layer adheres to this rule, and an explicit (pos zint N) with
# a numeral N in the input normalizes to N at the leaf.
#
# WHY. Using two nodes for zero and one -- &0/&1 and (pos zint &0)/(pos
# zint &1) -- the neutral and absorbing rules of symbolic-core and their
# zint equivalents both applied to every mixed form, yielding distinct
# result nodes: ((pos zint &1) * &1) was rewritten to &1 and also to
# (pos zint &1), meaning simp was two-valued. With a single node per
# value, there remains no room for overlap: symbolic-core's rules
# governing &0 and &1 ARE the rules for the integer zero and one, and
# this module needs no neutral or absorbing rules of its own.
#
# The power's exponent is a subterm just like any other, so an
# explicit (pos zint N) normalizes in that position as well:
# (x ^ (pos zint &2)) simplifies to (x ^ &2).
#
# NUMERIC FOLDING over Z needs no rules here at all: a red form over two
# signed numerals IS an ordinary +/-/* fact, the facade routes it to
# z+/z-/zx and re-exposes the result under = (lowered to the natural
# numeral when nonnegative), and symbolic-core's SN bridge adopts it --
# the exact mechanism of natural constant folding. This module only
# contributes what the facade cannot: leaf status, constant
# reassociation, negation of numerals, and the N -> Z promotions.

.import integer-arithmetic
.import symbolic-minus

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules ZL -- leaves, structured akin to symbolic-core's rules SB for
# cons lists (without this base case, parents of zint children would
# never undergo reduction). A zint is a leaf bearing its own reduced
# form, (Z red Z). When its magnitude is a numeral, it rewrites to the
# canonical integer corresponding to its value, a leaf and thus a
# normal form:
#   - (pos zint N) denotes the natural number N and rewrites to N in
#     canonical form;
#   - (neg zint N) rewrites to itself with N in canonical form, meaning
#     (neg zint <01>) and (neg zint &1) represent the same normal form;
#   - a zero magnitude, (neg zint &0) or (neg zint <00>), constitutes a
#     zero bearing a sign it cannot possess. No stack rule generates it,
#     yet as input it was formerly its own normal form, and the neutral
#     and absorbing rules met it with the natural zero: ((neg zint &0) +
#     &0) answered both (neg zint &0) and &0. It rewrites to the natural
#     &0.
# A zint whose magnitude is not a numeral undergoes no rewrite and
# remains its own normal form by virtue of the identity fallback, just
# as a list that is not a numeral does. A zint whose sign is neither pos
# nor neg lacks a reduced form and a normal form, much like an
# undeclared atom. The numeral test comes first because canon strips
# trailing zero cells from any list.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((pos zint N) needssimp (pos zint N)) => ((pos zint N) red (pos zint N))
((neg zint N) needssimp (neg zint N)) => ((neg zint N) red (neg zint N))
((G zint (A cons R)) needssimp (G zint (A cons R)))
=> ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))
((G zint (A cons R)) needssimp (G zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> ((A cons R) needscanon (A cons R))
((pos zint (A cons R)) needssimp (pos zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true,
 (A cons R) canonnum N)
=> ((pos zint (A cons R)) rw N)
((neg zint (A cons R)) needssimp (neg zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true,
 (A cons R) canonnum N, N != &0)
=> ((neg zint (A cons R)) rw (neg zint N))
((neg zint (A cons R)) needssimp (neg zint (A cons R)), (A cons R) isnumeral true,
 (A cons R) canonnum &0)
=> ((neg zint (A cons R)) rw &0)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules ZD1 -- Differentiation leaf (diff.zph rules DB): a zint numeral is
# a LEAF whose derivative is zero. Inert without diff.zph -- no dstate
# fact exists then -- exactly like symbolic-minus.zph's differentiation
# extension.
#
# The magnitude has to pass the numeral test defined by
# common-arithmetic. A zint whose magnitude fails to be a numeral
# resides beyond the vocabulary; without the guard, it was taken as a
# constant, and d(x * (pos zint x))/dx answered (pos zint x), where
# silence is owed -- this was also v1.0.1's answer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((G zint (A cons R)) dstate X) => ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))

((pos zint (A cons R)) dstate X, X ~ symvar, (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> (((pos zint (A cons R)) wrt X) deriv &0)

((neg zint (A cons R)) dstate X, X ~ symvar, (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> (((neg zint (A cons R)) wrt X) deriv &0)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules ZX -- constant reassociation over Z, the signed counterpart of
# symbolic-core's rules SX: (m * (n * u)) folds m and n into a single
# unit when at least one of them is negative (SX covers two naturals).
# Signed numerals by PATTERN, as in SX: (A cons R) represents a natural,
# (neg zint N) denotes a negative. The product K comes from the facade.
# As in SX, the guard is positioned on the RESULT: K equals &0 precisely
# when the natural factor does, which is the case the absorbing rules
# (X * &0) / (&0 * X) already answer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red ((neg zint M) * ((neg zint N) * U)))
=> ((neg zint M) * (neg zint N))
(T red ((neg zint M) * ((neg zint N) * U)),
 ((neg zint M) * (neg zint N)) = K, K != &1)
=> (T rw (K * U))

# (-1) * ((-1) * u) yields u: with K = &1, the rule above would
# reconstruct (&1 * u), and that expression is not in normal form --
# the neutral-element rule would still be applicable to it.
(T red ((neg zint &1) * ((neg zint &1) * U))) => (T rw U)

(T red ((neg zint M) * ((B cons S) * U)))
=> ((neg zint M) * (B cons S))
(T red ((neg zint M) * ((B cons S) * U)),
 ((neg zint M) * (B cons S)) = K, K != &0)
=> (T rw (K * U))

(T red ((A cons R) * ((neg zint N) * U)))
=> ((A cons R) * (neg zint N))
(T red ((A cons R) * ((neg zint N) * U)),
 ((A cons R) * (neg zint N)) = K, K != &0)
=> (T rw (K * U))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules ZN -- the natural magnitude of a negative numeral is obtained by
# negating it. (neg of (pos zint A)) needs no additional rule: the
# child simplifies to A first, and the rules ZP that follow handle the
# natural case.
#
# The magnitude must pass the numeral test. A zint whose magnitude
# fails to be a numeral is its own normal form (rules ZL), not a
# negative number, yet unwrapping it read it as one after all: (neg of
# (neg zint x)) simplified to x. A numeral magnitude in the reduced form
# is the canonical outcome generated by the leaf rules, thus the result
# is canonical.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red (neg of (neg zint (A cons R)))) => ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))
(T red (neg of (neg zint (A cons R))), (A cons R) isnumeral true) => (T rw (A cons R))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules ZP -- N -> Z promotion: complete natural partiality.
#
# Negation of a natural numeral has no natural value; here it becomes a
# zint. The zero case overlaps with symbolic-minus's (neg of &0) rule --
# deliberately conflict-free, both yield the natural &0.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red (neg of (A cons R))) => ((A cons R) cmp &0)
(T red (neg of (A cons R)), (A cons R) > &0) => (T rw (neg zint (A cons R)))
(T red (neg of (A cons R)), (A cons R) == &0) => (T rw &0)

# Natural subtraction with minuend < subtrahend derives no natural
# result (partiality by absence) -- so the SN fold is silent exactly
# where these rules fire, and vice versa: single-valuedness by case
# disjointness. The reversed subtraction is asserted as an ordinary -
# fact for the natural module to answer (MA1/MA2 cascade).
(T red ((A cons R) - (B cons S))) => ((A cons R) cmp (B cons S))
(T red ((A cons R) - (B cons S)), (A cons R) < (B cons S))
=> ((B cons S) - (A cons R))
(T red ((A cons R) - (B cons S)), (A cons R) < (B cons S),
 ((B cons S) - (A cons R)) = D)
=> (T rw (neg zint D))
